Sort an enum's members by name before generating hardware from them.

`EnumUInt` discovers its members with `Class.getDeclaredMethods`, which the JDK
documents as returning them "not sorted and ... not in any particular order" --
and which really does vary between runs. `validate()` keeps whatever order it
was handed, and `assertLegal` emits one comparison per value in that order:

    VecInit(this.values.map(_ === that)).asUInt.orR

So a reordering renames the FIRRTL nodes around it, and everything downstream
inherits new names: elaborating XiangShan twice produced 139 MB of
SystemVerilog differing in 216 wire names, with no design change behind it.
Nine elaborations here produced six distinct outputs; five with this patch
produced one. The cost is not cosmetic -- a generated artifact that changes for
no reason cannot be cached, and cannot be diffed when something real does
change.

Sorting is safe because the order was never meaningful. `values` is consumed by
`assertLegal`, which reduces it with `orR`; by `getValidSeq`, which pairs each
value with its name and is read by name; and by `validate`'s own checks, which
are order-independent. Nothing indexes it positionally. `getValuesString`, ten
lines above, already sorts before printing, so the file itself treats the
reflection order as arbitrary.

Worth upstreaming: every consumer of this class has the same problem, and none
of them can see it from where they sit.

diff --git a/src/main/scala/utils/EnumUInt.scala b/src/main/scala/utils/EnumUInt.scala
--- a/src/main/scala/utils/EnumUInt.scala
+++ b/src/main/scala/utils/EnumUInt.scala
@@ -113,7 +113,7 @@
         method.getReturnType == classOf[UInt] && // return UInt
         method.getParameterTypes.isEmpty         // no parameters
       }
-    val methods = methodsAll.filter(_.getName()(0).isUpper) // UpperCamelCase
+    val methods = methodsAll.filter(_.getName()(0).isUpper).sortBy(_.getName) // UpperCamelCase
     this.names = methods.map(_.getName).toSeq
     this.values = methods.map(_.invoke(this).asInstanceOf[UInt]).toSeq
     this.litValues = this.values.map(_.litValue)
